  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Apply the continuous-time Fourier series time-shifting property to convert coefficients for $x(t)$ into coefficients for $x(t - t0)$ by multiplying each $c_k$ by $e^{-jk omega0 t0}$ without recomputing the coefficient integral.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Shifting Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When a periodic signal is shifted in time, its continuous-time Fourier series coefficients keep the same magnitudes and change only in phase. The phase change depends on the harmonic index, so the shift affects each coefficient in a controlled way instead of changing the whole spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with a periodic signal $x(t)$ whose CTFS coefficients are $c_k$. If the shifted signal is $y(t)=x(t-t_0)$, with $t_0>0$ meaning a delay, then the new coefficients are $d_k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "d_k=c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The multiplier uses $k$, so each harmonic gets its own complex phase factor. The DC term is the special case $k=0$, where the multiplier becomes $1$ and $d_0=c_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So a time shift changes coefficient phase, not coefficient magnitude. Once you know the original $c_k$, you can update the coefficients for a delay or an advance directly, without recomputing the Fourier integrals.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Updating One Fourier Coefficient for a Delay",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For a signal with coefficient $c_2=3e^{j\\pi/4}$, find the corresponding coefficient $d_2$ for $y(t)=x(t-T_0/8)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is a delay, so use the CTFS time-shifting property instead of recomputing the coefficient integral. The magnitude stays the same; only the phase changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Here the relevant harmonic is $k=2$, and the shift is $t_0=T_0/8$. Since a periodic signal satisfies $\\omega_0T_0=2\\pi$, the phase multiplier for this harmonic is $e^{-j\\pi/2}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} d_2 &= c_2 e^{-j2\\omega_0(T_0/8)} \\\\ &= 3e^{j\\pi/4}e^{-j\\pi/2} \\\\ &= 3e^{-j\\pi/4} \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the updated coefficient is $d_2=3e^{-j\\pi/4}$. The coefficient magnitude is still $3$; only the phase has rotated.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Updating a Coefficient List for $x(t - t0)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $c_0=2$, $c_1=1-j$, and $c_2=4$ for $x(t)$, find $d_0$, $d_1$, and $d_2$ for $y(t)=x(t-T_0/4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a delay, so update the whole coefficient list with the time-shifting property. Since $y(t)=x(t-T_0/4)$ and $\\omega_0T_0=2\\pi$, the common shift angle is $\\omega_0t_0=\\pi/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} d_0 &= c_0 e^{-j0\\omega_0t_0} = 2, \\\\ d_1 &= c_1 e^{-j1\\omega_0t_0} = (1-j)e^{-j\\pi/2} = -1-j, \\\\ d_2 &= c_2 e^{-j2\\omega_0t_0} = 4e^{-j\\pi} = -4 \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The DC term stays unchanged because its multiplier is $1$. The nonzero harmonics each pick up their own phase rotation from the same shift angle.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the shifted coefficient list is $d_0=2$, $d_1=-1-j$, and $d_2=-4$. The result comes directly from the multiplier rule, without recomputing any Fourier integrals.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Shifting Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Handling an Advance as a Negative Time Shift",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $c_3=2e^{-j\\pi/3}$, find the $k=3$ coefficient of $y(t)=x(t+T_0/6)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the plus sign in $x(t+T_0/6)$. Rewrite it as $x(t-t_0)$ with $t_0=-T_0/6$, then apply the same CTFS time-shifting rule that you used for delays.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute the signed shift into $d_k=c_k e^{-jk\\omega_0 t_0}$. Since $\\omega_0T_0=2\\pi$, the phase for $k=3$ becomes $e^{-j3\\omega_0(-T_0/6)}=e^{j\\pi}$, which is a positive phase rotation because the shift is an advance.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned} d_3 &= c_3 e^{-j3\\omega_0 t_0} \\\\ &= 2e^{-j\\pi/3}e^{-j3\\omega_0(-T_0/6)} \\\\ &= 2e^{-j\\pi/3}e^{j\\pi} \\\\ &= 2e^{j2\\pi/3} \\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the coefficient is $d_3=2e^{j2\\pi/3}$. The advance is handled by the negative value of $t_0$; the multiplier still does all the work, and the magnitude stays the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
